  "topic_en": "Gauss relates theta-type series to the arithmetic-geometric mean via a sequence of squared series, then derives product expansions (pentagonal-number and related series) using a bracket notation $[x^n]$ for infinite products, apparently $\\prod(1-x^{nk})$.",
  "transcription": "222\nZitt: Y\n\n19. $(1+2x+2x^4)^4 = (1-2x+2x^4\\ldots)^4 + (2x^{\\frac14}+2x^{\\frac94}+)^4$\nAnwendung auf arithm. geom. Mittel\n\n20. Also $a = h(1+2x+2x^4)^2$ $\\quad b = h(1-2x+2x^4)^2$ $\\quad c = \\sqrt{(aa-bb)}$\n$a' = h(1+2xx+2x^8)^2$ $\\quad b' = h(1-2x^2+2x^8)^2$ $\\quad c' = \\sqrt{(a'a'-b'b')} = \\frac12(a-b) = \\frac{cc}{4a'}$\n$a'' = h(1+2x^4+2x^{16})^2$ $\\quad b'' = h(1-2x^4+2x^{16})^2$ $\\quad c'' = \\sqrt{(a''a''-b''b'')} = \\frac12(a'-b') = \\frac{c'c'}{4a''}$\n$a''' = h(1+2x^8+2x^{32})^2$ $\\quad b''' = h(1-2x^8+2x^{32})^2$ $\\quad c''' = \\sqrt{(a'''a'''-b'''b''')} = \\frac12(a''-b'') = \\frac{c''c''}{4a'''}$\n&c. &c.\n\n$c' = \\frac{cc}{4a'}$ $\\quad \\sqrt{\\frac{c'}{4h}} = \\frac{c}{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a'h}}$\n$c'' = \\frac{c^4}{64a'a'a''}$ $\\quad \\sqrt[4]{\\frac{c''}{4h}} = \\frac{c}{4.a'^{\\frac12}a''^{\\frac14}h^{\\frac14}}$\n$c''' = \\frac{c^8}{2^{14}a'^4a''^2a'''}$ $\\quad \\sqrt[8]{\\frac{c'''}{4h}} = \\frac{c}{4a'^{\\frac12}a''^{\\frac14}a'''^{\\frac18}h^{\\frac18}}$\n&c.\n\n$b'' \\; -\\frac12 \\qquad -\\frac12$\n$b''' \\; \\frac32-\\frac14 \\qquad \\frac54$\n$b^{IV} \\; -1+\\frac34-\\frac18 \\qquad -\\frac38$\n$b^{V} \\; -\\frac12+\\frac38-\\frac1{16} \\qquad -\\frac3{16}$\n\n$\\left(\\frac{b'''^3}{b''b^{IV}b^{IV}}\\right)^{\\frac12}$ $\\quad \\frac{b^{IV}b^{IV}b^{IV}}{b'''b^{V}b^{V}}^{\\frac14}$\n\n21. $x^{\\frac14} = \\frac{c}{4a'^{\\frac12}a''^{\\frac14}a'''^{\\frac18}\\ldots} = \\frac{c}{4a'}\\left(\\frac{a'}{a''}\\right)^{\\frac12}\\left(\\frac{a''}{a'''}\\right)^{\\frac14}\\left(\\frac{a'''}{a^{IV}}\\right)^{\\frac18}$ &c.\n\n$\\left(\\frac{b'''b'''}{b''a'''}\\right)^{\\frac12}$ $\\quad \\frac{b^{IV}b^{IV}}{b'''a^{IV}}^{\\frac14}$\n\n22. $x = \\frac{a-b}{8a''}\\left(\\frac{a''}{a'''}\\right)^{\\frac12}\\left(\\frac{a'''}{a^{IV}}\\right)^{\\frac14}$ &c.\n\nMan kann auch erst st. $y$, $xy$ setzen\n\nSetzt man in 6 statt $x$, $x^3$ u. statt $y$, $-x$ so wird\n$(1-xx)(1-x^{10})(1-x^{16})\\ldots(1-x^2)(1-x^8)(1-x^{14}) = \\frac{1}{[x^6]}\\{1-xx-x^4+x^{10}+x^{14}-x^{24}-x^{30}+ \\&c.\\}$\noder\n[23] $[x] = 1-x-xx+x^5+x^7-x^{12}-x^{15}+$ &c.\n\nAndere Beweis[?]\nMan setze in 6 statt $x$, $x^3$ u. st. $y$, $+x$ so wird\n[24] $1+x+xx+x^5+x^7+x^{12}+x^{15}+$ &c. $= [x^3](1+[-x-])(1+xx)(1+x^4)(1+x^5) = \\frac{[xx][x^3]^2}{[x][x^6]}$\nMan setze in 6 statt $x$, $x^3$ u. st. $y$, [-x-] $xxy$ so wird\n$1+\\frac{x}{y}+x^5y+\\frac{x^8}{yy}+x^{16}yy+$ &c. $= (1+\\frac{x}{y})(1+\\frac{x^7}{y})(1+\\frac{x^{13}}{y})\\ldots(1+x^5y)(1+x^{11}y)(1+x^{17}y)\\ldots[x^6]$\noder statt $x$, $x^3$ gesetzt\n$[-x-]+\\frac{x^4}{y}+x^{16}y+\\frac{x^{25}}{yy}+x^{49}yy+$ &c. $= x(1+\\frac{x^3}{y})(1+\\frac{x^{21}}{y})(1+\\frac{x^{39}}{y})\\ldots(1+x^{15}y)(1+x^{33}y)(1+x^{51}y)\\ldots[x^{18}]$\nAlso\n[25] $x+x^4+x^{16}+x^{25}+x^{49}+$ &c. $= x(1+x^3)(1+x^{15})(1+x^{21})(1+x^{33})(1+x^{39})\\ldots[x^{18}]$\n$= x\\frac{[x^6]^2 \\times [x^9][x^{36}]}{[x^3][x^{12}][x^{18}]}$\n\n[26] $x-x^4-x^{16}+x^{25}+x^{49}+$ &c. $= x(1-x^3)(1-x^{15})(1-x^{21})(1-x^{33})\\ldots[x^{18}]$\n$= x\\frac{[x^3][x^{18}]^2}{[x^6].[x^9]}$",
